#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193653} :activity/id #uuid "02255835-1ac4-5d6f-b968-e337f6d42745" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048343 :topic/id #uuid "96874e3b-8b97-5c33-995d-d34a9d3daf21" :topic/math-academy-id 4077} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193882 :tutorial/content "Recall that $H\\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ is a **subspace** of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nIn the vector space $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ there are two **trivial subspaces**.\n\n- One trivial subspace is the set \n\n$$\nH=\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}\n$$\n\n consisting of just the zero vector. This is a subspace because the sum of two zero vectors is the zero vector, and any multiple of the zero vector is the zero vector.\n\n- Another trivial subspace is the set \n\n$$\nH = \\mathbb{R}^n\n$$\n\n consisting of the entire set $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ This is a subspace because the sum of two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ and any multiple of a vector in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "01ed4d92-df35-5f3c-aa41-43246f6ea9e9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6854 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "93a1b04d-d02e-5aae-bd94-6c2a571d80d7" :step/math-academy-id 41649 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193657}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193654 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192510 :question/id #uuid "32f7b0e6-5a68-52ab-9d86-ea71387cfb77" :question/math-academy-id "e-10975"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3fe3596-340e-5155-bed3-cf25f5843d70" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051052}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323709}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space"} :step/id #uuid "f42bb3f4-1e7f-53f5-ad85-ca2e942d30b3" :step/math-academy-id 41650 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193658}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192787 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192511 :question/id #uuid "554fc7b1-62ca-5189-bda1-712f518ad617" :question/math-academy-id "e-10976"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050373}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342197}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces"} :step/id #uuid "4c579fcb-af17-53b9-89c4-b7c4d86d6a8f" :step/math-academy-id 41653 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193659}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192788 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192512 :question/id #uuid "6e80df98-c67c-5f27-a44b-d0bc8f681ac6" :question/math-academy-id "e-10979"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a8dbbab-2dea-5fff-86f0-3411f5405ee9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317355}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space"} :step/id #uuid "68866355-7603-545f-b26a-1a0cee48c958" :step/math-academy-id 41665 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193655}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193883 :tutorial/content "In $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n> *The only non-trivial subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ are straight lines that pass through the origin.*\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows a subspace in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n![](images/3c/3c0ea9584ae3229a7930c6ef2b53031770a7190b980b5b595cc233cae29ea49d.png)\n\nLet's now consider a few examples of subsets of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ that are *not* subspaces of $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\n![](images/5a/5aac9808e9196653ee54974b495443a95d8913cd0b220a6893acb68ff3b967bb.png)\n\n- Diagram I shows a line that does not pass through the origin. It is *not* a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $2\\mathbf v$ does not. So the set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Diagram II is not a subspace because $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf v$ belong to the set while $\\mathbf u+\\mathbf v$ does not. So the set is not closed under vector addition.\n\n- Diagram III is not a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $-\\mathbf v$ does not. So the set is not closed under scalar multiplication." :tutorial/id #uuid "33c42a7a-a5c2-5b36-a839-4fab6351c9e4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6857 :tutorial/title "Non-Trivial Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space"} :step/id #uuid "18e8a405-fc8d-5344-9133-d42b106e46e6" :step/math-academy-id 41670 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193654}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193884 :tutorial/content "In $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n> *The only non-trivial subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ are straight lines or planes that pass through the origin.*\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows two subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n![](images/57/57b6625b607537d1c90e849d6ee6ac8480648aa4ad6e3a357bedc8d8408256ad.png)\n\nOn the other hand, the sets shown in the diagrams below are *not* subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n![](images/f4/f4fbaee302e1c25e740f4164841a674243a43197b0f20ddb35d2d41bb4175b68.png)\n\n- Diagram I shows a plane that does not pass through the origin. It is not a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $2\\mathbf v$ does not. So, the set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Diagram II shows a line that does not pass through the origin. It is not a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $2\\mathbf v$ does not. So, the set is not closed under scalar multiplication." :tutorial/id #uuid "8d9802f0-c392-5e37-afb4-72f73451813e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6861 :tutorial/title "Non-Trivial Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space"} :step/id #uuid "40d83f58-34e5-5b7b-b300-54ae33e99a59" :step/math-academy-id 41676 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193656}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193659 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193885 :tutorial/content "Lastly, consider the set $\\mathbb{R},$ which belongs to the family of vector spaces $\\mathbb{R}^n$ (when $n=1$). This vector space has only two subspaces, both of which are trivial:\n\n- the zero subspace $\\{0\\},$ which contains only the number $0,$ and\n\n- the whole space $\\mathbb{R}$ (or, formally, $\\mathbb{R}^1$), which contains all the real numbers." :tutorial/id #uuid "fb0671eb-0c5d-59dc-a3f9-cda72b4da034" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6862 :tutorial/title "Final Notes About the Set of Real Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "fda08207-59ef-5481-816d-d4abed842b3a" :step/math-academy-id 41677}] :activity/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space: Geometric Interpretation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192926}]}
